From b2117be2e327ac98a890a47037cacc820f7a2447 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 13:12:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 189/190] hir_typeck: an opaque projection's associated type
 follows a binding on a supertrait in its bound

Expanding `<<D::Node<'_> as SgNode<'_>>::erased#children_0 as Iterator>::Item`
looks at the bounds of the hidden `erased#children_0` type, here
`ExactSizeIterator<Item = Self>`.  The inner-bounds step used a binding
only when the bound's trait was the queried trait; for a supertrait it
walked to `Iterator` through the supertrait path, which carries no
binding, and then searched impls, so the projection stayed opaque.

ast-grep-config 0.45.3 (ast-grep 0.45.3) rule/nth_child.rs:235
`parent.children().filter(..)`: ast-grep-core's `Node::children` returns
`impl ExactSizeIterator + '_` with the hidden type `Map<that projection,
closure>`, and `Map<..>: Iterator` needs `closure: FnMut(<projection as
Iterator>::Item)`, which never matched the closure's impl: "No
applicable methods for {Map<..>}.filter".  Inside ast-grep-core the same
check passed because the closure's argument type was still an inference
variable there.

A binding whose source trait is the queried trait (same arguments) is
used, monomorphised with the projection's type as `Self`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
